#{[{:activity/expected-seconds 720.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186116120} :activity/id #uuid "3b658871-23f8-5b8d-92ee-5294fec9bba3" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048445} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186116120 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186116493 :tutorial/content "We can use Gaussian elimination to find the general solutions of systems with any number of equations and variables. For instance, consider the system of linear equations\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x-y+z &=1, \\\\ y+3z &=0.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nThis system has the augmented matrix\n\n\n$$\nM=\\left[\\begin{array}{ccc|c}1 & -1 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 3 & 0\\end{array}\\right].\n$$\n\n\n\nUsing the standard Gauss-Jordan method, we can reduce this matrix to reduced row echelon form as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 3 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\, & {R}_{1} & :={R}_{1}+{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 4 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 3 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe corresponding system is now\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x+4z &=1 \\\\ y+3z &=0.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nWe see that the basic variables are $x$ and $y,$ and we have one free variable $z.$ From the second equation, we get $y=-3z,$ and from the first equation, we obtain $x=1-4z.$ Therefore, the general solution is\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x &=1-4z, \\\\ y &=-3z, \\\\ z &=z.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nIn vector form, the solution can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}1-4z \\\\ -3z \\\\ z\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}+z\\begin{bmatrix}-4 \\\\ -3 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix},\\,z\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).\\end{aligned}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "3eff050b-e4ba-52e5-aa10-7bba2feef2fc" :tutorial/math-academy-id 3162 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "6022e9b9-60ab-5ac5-b16a-17583fa649c3" :step/math-academy-id 17213 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186116121}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186116121 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115241 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115070 :question/id #uuid "dc6dd9b4-de7f-52d0-8ff9-a7a7f7a9abd7" :question/math-academy-id "e-4711" :question/problem "Consider the system of linear equations\n\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x+5y+z &=2 \\\\ x+5y+2z &=5.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\n\nUse the standard Gauss-Jordan method to reduce the augmented matrix $M$ of the system to reduced row echelon form, and then solve the linear system." :question/worked-solution "The augmented matrix for this system is\n\n\n$$\nM= \\left[ \\begin{array}{ccc|c}1 & 5 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 5 & 2 & 5\\end{array} \\right].\n$$\n\n\n\nUsing the standard Gauss-Jordan method, we can reduce this matrix to reduced row echelon form as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 5 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 5 & 2 & 5\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-1){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 5 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & 3\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{1} & :={R}_{1}+(-1){R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 5 & 0 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & 3\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe corresponding system is now\n\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x+5y &=-1 \\\\ z &=3.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\n\n\nThe basic variables are the variables whose coefficients are pivots. In this case, the basic variables are $x$ and $z,$ and we have one free variable, $y.$ That means we need to find $x$ and $z$ in terms of $y.$ \n\nTherefore, the general solution is\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x &=-1-5y \\\\ y &=y \\\\ z &=3,\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nwhere $y$ (the free variable) is an arbitrary number.\n\nIn vector form, the solution can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-1-5y \\\\ y \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix},\\,y\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "2d81e3f1-d930-59ee-b439-2a3645aebb6c" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048742}] :knowledge-point/question-generator {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186557573} :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115751} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115830} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186266111} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186266112} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186302108} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186302193} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186326924} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186400625} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186402009} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186402756} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186417081} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186557520} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186557532} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186557536} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186557548} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186557549} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186557551} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186557557} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186557558} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186557567} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186557569} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186569961} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186569978} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186642307}] :knowledge-point/title "Solving a Consistent System of Equations Using Gauss-Jordan Elimination"} :step/id #uuid "79bb6bd1-8c36-5f56-9575-8a1b8388eb57" :step/math-academy-id 17214 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186116122}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186116122 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186116494 :tutorial/content "Recall that a system of linear equations is called **consistent** if it has at least one solution. Otherwise, if there are no solutions, the system is called **inconsistent**. \n\nFor a consistent system, \n\n- the system is called **consistent independent** if the solution is unique, while\n\n- the system is called **consistent dependent** if there are infinitely many solutions.\n\n![](images/f5/f5b808eac6e1d865b13b68ce2a4f7d317560ae3965928e478531fcd2148c2c6b.png)" :tutorial/id #uuid "e0c85152-daea-5a87-a3ba-81870558c7a2" :tutorial/math-academy-id 3163 :tutorial/title "Consistent and Inconsistent Systems of Linear Equations"} :step/id #uuid "2fa6eba1-6298-51b5-bbc9-bd8c46a79468" :step/math-academy-id 17215 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186116209}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186116123 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115242 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115071 :question/id #uuid "f2c7cfd3-da43-5208-bd0e-21692357fabe" :question/math-academy-id "e-4712" :question/problem "Determine the value of $k$ for which the following system of linear equations is inconsistent:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}2x-2y &=1, \\\\ 4x+ky &=-3\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "The augmented matrix for this system is\n\n\n\n$$\nM= \\left[ \\begin{array}{cc|c}2 & -2 & 1 \\\\ 4 & k & -3\\end{array} \\right].\n$$\n\n\n\nUsing the method of Gaussian elimination, we can reduce this matrix to row echelon form as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -2 & 1 \\\\ 4 & k & -3\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}-2{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & k+4 & -5\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe corresponding system is now \n\n\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}2x-2y &=1 \\\\ (k+4)y &=-5.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\n\n\nThe system is inconsistent if it has no solutions. For the system to have no solutions, one of the equations above must be false.\n\nIf $k=-4,$ then the second equation is $0=-5,$ which is false, and the system will have no solution.\n\nTherefore, our system is inconsistent when $k=-4.$"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "22733f71-de4a-5058-a41c-039e66e333c5" :knowledge-point/question-generator {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186557445} :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115753} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115831} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186266116} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186266117} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186266118} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186266119} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186266120} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186302109} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186302143} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186344961} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186344969} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186344971} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186398982} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186417082} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186425949} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186492874} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186557414} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186557415} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186557416} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186557419} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186557444} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186580229}] :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Value of a Constant for Which a Given Linear System Is Consistent or Inconsistent"} :step/id #uuid "1d1a97f8-c872-5285-b963-d1938948df37" :step/math-academy-id 17216} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186116209 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115299 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115124 :question/id #uuid "cd56314d-f45a-55be-9762-983fe507a1a0" :question/math-academy-id "e-6173" :question/problem "Given the system\n\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}2x-3y &=4 \\\\ -4x+6y &=-8,\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\n\nwhich of the following statements are true?\n\n- The system is consistent dependent\n\n- The system has no solution\n\n- The system is consistent independent" :question/worked-solution "To classify the system, we first need to find its solution. The augmented matrix for this system is\n\n\n$$\nM= \\left[ \\begin{array}{cc|c}2 & -3 & 4 \\\\ -4 & 6 & -8\\end{array} \\right].\n$$\n\n\n\nUsing Gaussian elimination, we can reduce this matrix to row echelon form as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -3 & 4 \\\\ -4 & 6 & -8\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+2{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -3 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe system is now\n\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}2x-3y &=4 \\\\ 0x+0y &=0.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\n\n\nSince any numbers $x$ and $y$ form a solution of the final equation $0x+0y=0,$ we can remove this equation from the system. So, our system now consists of just a single equation:\n\n$$\n2x -3y= 4\n$$\n\nThe basic variable is $x,$ and we have a free variable $y.$ That means we need to find $x$ in terms of $y.$ \n\nTherefore, since there are infinitely many solutions, we conclude that the system is *consistent dependent*.\n\nAs a result, only statement I is true."} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "f9469d5c-e097-590c-8ddb-2c7fda353fac" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050240}] :knowledge-point/question-generator {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186523192} :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115752} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115832} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186266113} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186266114} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186266115} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186344956} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186344957} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186363108} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186364365} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186400632} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186400639} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186400646} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186402763} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186403787} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186492840} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186523101} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186523112} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186523119} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186523120} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186523134} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186523146} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186523149} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186523165} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186523181} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186523190} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186569976} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186580307}] :knowledge-point/title "Classifying a System as Consistent Dependent, Consistent Independent, or Inconsistent"} :step/id #uuid "e80cfcd9-1219-52d8-96c7-0ee06cdf52fc" :step/math-academy-id 23072 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186116123}}] :activity/title "Gaussian Elimination For NxM Systems of Equations" :activity/type {:db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186115479}]}
